A||||8||Sage||RACK REPAIR||||1||
Z||000000||Sage||08-03-2000||09:39 PM||||Has anyone ever replaced the inner tie rod ends on their steering rack?  I have a rack with good tie rod ends,(inner and outer)but a bad rack assembly, and one with a good rack but bad tie rod ends.  Is it possible to change the tie rods without special tools?<P>Took the boots off and removed the plastic sleeve covering the inner tie rod ends, but it looks like the collar is crimped on and can't be easily replaced at least without special tools or knowledge.<P>Does anybody know anything about this?<P>TIA for any responses, and am hoping someone has done this, or knows how they are put together.<P>Have a good one!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000001||PJ||08-03-2000||09:52 PM||||Its pretty easy if you use a small chisel to open up the crimped parts, then a pipe wrench to to twist it off.<P>PJ<BR>ITA #66 Black||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||08-03-2000||10:31 PM||||If you're talking about the metal band that is crimped to hold the rubber boot on, cut those off with a pair of dikes. I used a large worm gear hose clamp to reinstall it. Others have used nylon wire ties. The proper sized large wrench will work to unscrew the inner tie rod from the rack (you'll see a couple flat spots designed for the wrench). I suppose a pipe wrench would work in a pinch. There actually is a special inner tie rod tool, but I used regular tools last time I did this.||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||08-04-2000||08:36 PM||||Don't bother. Odds are that after all of the work you will discover the rack and pinion gears are worn. Get another rack.<P>I started the same project. The rack rod was shot and the pinion was worn pretty good.<P>Autozone was selling them for $114 ea.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Rodney||08-04-2000||09:16 PM||||Remember that you can not get a new rack or pinion gear for a Fiero steering rack. All the rebuilders can do is fix up good used ones. Biggest problem is the outer passenger side bushing. As it wears the wear on the rack and pinion gear is accelerated. So replace that bushing before the rack and pinion gear is worn beyond reuse.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||08-05-2000||12:23 AM||||Yes, I should have said the complete rack is $114. Many others are $250+. I talked with a few rebuilders and they confirmed what Rodney is saying. They are down to scrounging good rack rods and pinion gears to rebuild the units.<P>Translations? Get one now.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Sage||08-05-2000||09:55 AM||||Thanks everyone.  This is what I suspected, but just thought I would ask, before I started making metal chips.  Autozone here has them for $114.00 also, but it is a rebuilt rack.<BR> Guess, they are gonna get like the cars themselves, and real estate, "they ain't makin no more!"  I just thought if I could make one out of the two I have, it would save $114.00!<P>Thanks again for all the input.<BR>  Got one more that is perfect except the housing on the pass. side is broke.  Am going to see if I can have someone heli-arc it.<BR>Everybody have a good one!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||Rodney||08-05-2000||12:17 PM||||I heard you can still get them new from GM but they are very expensive.||||reg||1
Z||000008||terryk||08-05-2000||04:11 PM||||The pinion and rack are NS (not servicable) from GM. Part of the complete rack assembly.<P>I've been thinking that the 80/81 X car rack might work. There are PS and non-PS versions. Not that I haven't considered power steering when I install the DOHC, but I grabbed two rebuilt Fiero racks, so .....<BR>0<BR>||||reg||1
